      {
        "date": "2026-10-07",
        "title": "Avia Solutions Group (ASG Finance DAC) — AskB Credit Summary & Distressed Dislocation Analysis",
        "author": "AskB / CEMBI Corporate Desk",
        "recommendation": "Hold / Selective Yield Play (~15.28% YTM, +1,052 bps spread)",
        "content": "### Issuer Snapshot & Market Pricing\nASG Finance DAC is the Irish-domiciled financing vehicle for Avia Solutions Group (ASG) PLC, the world's largest ACMI (Aircraft, Crew, Maintenance and Insurance) provider, headquartered in Dublin and controlled by Lithuanian entrepreneur Gediminas Ziemelis. The group operates a fleet of ~221 aircraft with an ambition to reach ~700 by 2030.\n\nThe sole outstanding bond is the AVIASG 9.75% May 2029 ($300m senior unsecured note, ISIN: US00217AAB26 / XS2815876920).\n• **S&P Rating:** BB- (last action Aug 2020)\n• **Fitch IDR:** BB / Outlook Negative (revised Mar 2026) | Recovery Rating: RR4\n• **Moody's:** Not rated\n• **Current Yield / Spread:** ~15.28% YTM / ~1,052 bps spread.\nAt ~1,050 bps over, the bond is trading well into distressed territory — a significant dislocation from its BB/BB- ratings, reflecting credit-negative events since late 2025.\n\n### Financial Reporting Context\nBloomberg's structured financial data for this private entity is only available through FY2022 (entity-level). More recent operational data (FY2024 EBITDA +17%, Q1 2025 revenues EUR 534m) has been disclosed via Euronext Dublin filings. FY2025 Annual Report was published April 27, 2026.\n\n### Chronological Credit Developments (2025–2026)\n• **Nov 2025 — SmartLynx Allegations & Bondholder Selloff:** Bonds sold off sharply following allegations tied to ASG's sale of former subsidiary SmartLynx Airlines. Company convened emergency bondholder call on Dec 1, 2025, calling it a 'smear campaign'.\n• **Feb 2025 — 80 Boeing 737 MAX Order:** ASG signed deal with Boeing for 80 737 MAX jets to support fleet tripling target.\n• **Mar 2026 — Fitch Outlook Cut to Negative:** Fitch affirmed Long-Term IDR at BB but revised Outlook to Negative from Stable. Senior notes affirmed at BB / RR4.\n• **Apr 2026 — Bonds Fall to ~79.6 cents:** 9.75% 2029 notes fell into distressed territory (low of 79.6c) as Middle East conflict weighed on airline capacity and wet-lease utilisation.\n• **Feb 2026 — Subsidiary Restructuring:** KlasJet and AirExplore airlines merged with associated job cuts in Lithuania.\n• **Jun 2026 — Helisota Liquidation:** Helicopter maintenance subsidiary Helisota approved for liquidation by ASG as sole shareholder.\n• **Jul 2026 — New CEO Appointed:** Zilvinas Lapinskas appointed CEO of Avia Solutions Group.\n• **Jul 2026 — BAA Training Capex Write-off:** BAA Training terminated 2023 Cessna Skyhawk deal (48 aircraft), writing off EUR 1.5m advance; had negative equity and required EUR 29m capital injection from ASG in July 2025.\n\n### Credit Positives vs Negatives\n**Positives:**\n1. World's largest ACMI operator — structural market position with high barriers to entry and long-term airline customer relationships.\n2. FY2024 EBITDA grew 17% YoY; Q1 2025 revenues EUR 534m (+5% YoY) confirm top-line scale.\n3. Fitch affirmed BB rating — no downgrade to sub-investment grade despite Negative Outlook.\n4. Gross debt reduction and cash build suggest deleveraging at the consolidated level.\n\n**Negatives & Watch Items:**\n1. Severe spread dislocation (~1,050 bps) implies the market prices in meaningful default risk beyond rating.\n2. Fitch Negative Outlook: downgrade to BB- would push notes to the cusp of single-B.\n3. Governance & SmartLynx overhang: allegations have not fully cleared.\n4. Large capex commitment: 80 Boeing 737 MAX order creates multi-billion dollar commitment.\n5. Subsidiary stress: BAA Training and Helisota liquidation signal pockets of group cash bleed."
      }
